# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
  "base": [
    ("Why a paver patio's base isn't the same as a driveway's",
     "<p>Patios and driveways both sit on a compacted aggregate base, but the two bases aren't built to the same spec. ICPI Tech Spec 2 calls for at least 4 inches of compacted base under a patio or walkway on well-drained soil, compared with at least 6 inches under a residential driveway, because a dining set and a grill load the ground far more lightly than a parked car. We still compact that base in lifts and check it against 98 percent of standard Proctor density before the bedding sand goes down. Skip that step on a {city} lot and the first rainy season finds every soft spot for you.</p>"),
    ("Grading a patio to slope away from the house",
     "<p>Before any base goes in, we set the finished grade so water runs away from the foundation and off the paver field, typically a slope of about 1 inch for every 8 to 10 feet of patio. That matters here: {city} sees most of its roughly 50 inches of annual rain packed into a rainy season running from late May into October, often as a hard afternoon downpour rather than a gentle soak. A patio graded flat, or tipped back toward the house, holds that water against the slider or the lanai threshold instead of carrying it into the yard. Getting the slope right in the base is cheaper than fixing it after the pavers are down.</p>"),
    ("Base work where a screen enclosure sits on the patio",
     "<p>When a pool cage or screen enclosure shares the same patio, its footers are a different animal from the paver field around them. Enclosure posts typically land on isolated concrete footers set below the compacted base, not on the paver base itself, so the paver courses can settle or be lifted for maintenance without disturbing the structure overhead. On a {city} job we lay out those footer locations before compacting the surrounding base, because cutting an isolated footing through finished aggregate later wastes a day and leaves a patch that compacts differently than the base around it. Coordinating the two scopes up front keeps the whole patio behaving as one surface.</p>"),
    ("Flatwoods soil and why the base sometimes gets thicker",
     "<p>A lot of the territory we work in sits on flatwoods soils such as Myakka, Florida's official state soil, where a seasonal high water table can sit within 18 inches of the surface for part of the year. ICPI's guidance is direct about what that means for a paver base: continually wet or weak soil needs a base 2 to 4 inches thicker than the standard patio or walkway depth. On a {city} lot with poor natural drainage, that extra aggregate, plus a perforated drain pipe where the soil stays saturated, does more for the long-term flatness of the patio than any amount of extra paver thickness would.</p>"),
    ("Why ridge sand doesn't get a free pass on base work",
     "<p>On the sandy ridge soils found west of Orlando, in series like Candler, Astatula and Tavares, water drains so fast that the ground rarely holds a puddle, and some installers assume that means the base can be thinner. It can't. Florida's residential code still limits how deep fill can go without engineering review: no more than 24 inches of clean sand or gravel, or 8 inches of earth fill. Excessively drained sand also shifts underfoot more than people expect once pavers carry chairs and daily foot traffic, so a {city} patio on ridge sand still gets full-depth, properly compacted aggregate rather than a thin leveling layer over native sand.</p>"),
    ("Base under a walkway versus a full patio",
     "<p>A garden walkway or a run of stepping pavers from the driveway to the front door doesn't need the same base program as a 400-square-foot patio, but it still needs one. ICPI's 4-inch minimum compacted base for pedestrian areas applies to a 3-foot-wide path the same way it applies to an open patio, because a narrow walkway with no base shifts and dishes faster than a wide one, since there's less paver mass holding each course in line. On a {city} property where a walkway crosses a soft, shaded side yard, we'll often add a touch more base depth there than the sunny, well-drained stretch of the same path.</p>"),
    ("Keeping downspouts from undermining the base",
     "<p>A paver base is compacted aggregate, and compacted aggregate erodes if a gutter downspout dumps directly onto it year after year. We route downspout discharge away from the patio edge, or under it through solid pipe to daylight past the base line, before the aggregate goes in, rather than leaving a splash block to fight a concentrated stream of roof water during every storm. On a {city} roof shedding its share of around 50 inches of annual rain, an unmanaged downspout at the patio edge washes fines out of the base within a few seasons, and the pavers nearest that corner settle first.</p>"),
    ("Tying a new paver base into an old concrete slab",
     "<p>Extending an existing concrete patio or stoop with a paver field is really a base-matching exercise. We saw-cut a clean edge, excavate to a depth that lets the new compacted aggregate and bedding sand meet the old slab's surface flush, and set an edge restraint where the paver field butts the concrete instead of letting sand run up against it. Get that elevation wrong on a {city} addition and the seam telegraphs as a trip edge within a year, because the two materials settle at different rates. A rigid edge restraint anchored into the paver base, not into the old slab, keeps that joint from opening up.</p>"),
  ],
  "build": [
    ("Picking a paver thickness for a patio that only sees foot traffic",
     "<p>ICPI sets 60 mm (2.375 inches) as the standard thickness for pedestrian areas and residential driveways, reserving 80 mm (3.125 inches) units for streets and industrial pavement. A patio in {city} that only carries foot traffic, patio furniture and the occasional wheelbarrow doesn't need the heavier unit some homeowners request after seeing it quoted for a driveway elsewhere. Going thicker than the job calls for mostly adds cost without adding durability where the load never approaches vehicle weight. What does more for long-term performance on a foot-traffic surface is a properly compacted base and a snug joint, not a thicker unit sitting on a thin one.</p>"),
    ("Travertine pavers versus concrete pavers for a patio",
     "<p>Travertine and concrete pavers solve the same problem with different materials. Travertine is a natural stone that stays noticeably cooler underfoot in direct sun than a dark concrete paver, which matters on an uncovered {city} patio in summer, though we won't put a number on the temperature difference since no verified measurement exists for Florida conditions. It's also more porous, so sealing matters more over time. Market ranges for installed paver patios run about $10 to $17 per square foot, and travertine typically prices above the top of that range because of material cost. Concrete pavers come in more colors and patterns and cost less to replace if one cracks.</p>"),
    ("Why edge restraint isn't optional on a patio",
     "<p>Every paver field needs a continuous edge restraint around the perimeter and anywhere the paving material changes, whether that's plastic, aluminum or steel. The spikes that hold it down drive into the compacted aggregate base, not into the native soil beneath it, since soil alone won't hold a spike against the outward push of thousands of interlocking pavers. A strip of plastic landscape edging meant for a flower bed is not a substitute; it isn't rated for that job. On a {city} patio bordered by turf or mulch, a proper restraint is what keeps the outer courses from creeping outward season after season until the edge joints open up.</p>"),
    ("Bedding sand and joint sand aren't the same material",
     "<p>Bedding sand is the thin layer the pavers actually sit in, screeded to an uncompacted 1 inch before a single unit goes down. Joint sand is swept into the narrow gaps between pavers afterward and compacted in with a plate compactor, and ICPI specifies joints no wider than about 1/8 inch. Using the wrong sand in either layer, or skipping the compaction pass that locks joint sand in place, is one of the more common reasons a {city} patio feels loose or individual pavers rock underfoot within the first year. Getting both layers right is a small part of the job in material cost and a large part of it in feel.</p>"),
    ("Choosing a pattern for a patio instead of a driveway",
     "<p>A paver pattern on a patio does a different job than the same pattern on a driveway. Herringbone resists the shifting caused by turning tires, a force a dining table and a few chairs never apply, so a {city} patio has more room to run a running bond, basketweave or soldier-course border purely for the look homeowners want rather than for load distribution. We still keep cut pavers out of high-wear zones like the path from the back door to the grill, since small cut pieces are the first to work loose regardless of the overall pattern. The edge restraint, not the pattern, carries most of the structural work on a pedestrian surface.</p>"),
    ("Building a walkway or a run of stepping pavers",
     "<p>A connecting walkway, say from a {city} driveway to the front porch, usually runs 3 to 4 feet wide, narrow enough that a full paver base and bedding sand bed still apply but the material list shrinks fast. Large-format stepping pavers set individually in a lawn, spaced by a comfortable stride, are a lighter-duty option for an informal path across a side yard, each unit bedded in its own patch of sand and gravel rather than a continuous paver field. The two approaches use the same base principles at different scales, and mixing them, a formal paver walkway near the house that transitions to stepping pavers farther out, is a common way to control cost over a longer path.</p>"),
    ("Adding a paver-set fire pit to the patio",
     "<p>A fire pit built into a paver patio needs its own base detail: a noncombustible pad, often a ring of concrete or extra-compacted aggregate under the fire pit itself, and enough clearance from the house, any screen enclosure and overhanging trees that heat and embers aren't a problem. We set the fire pit's paver course level with the surrounding field but treat the base under it as a separate, denser section rather than an extension of the standard patio base. On a {city} patio where the fire pit sits near a wood fence or landscaping, that clearance distance matters as much as the base spec underneath it.</p>"),
    ("Where a screen enclosure track meets the paver field",
     "<p>A pool cage or screen room usually has an aluminum track anchored to its own footer, and the paver field has to meet that track cleanly without trapping water against it. We set a control joint there in practice, a slightly wider sand joint backed by a rigid edge restraint, right where the pavers meet the enclosure's base plate, so the two systems move independently without cracking the enclosure's footer or heaving the nearest paver course. On a {city} lanai addition, matching that transition elevation precisely keeps the screen door swinging freely instead of dragging on a paver that settled a quarter inch high.</p>"),
  ],
  "process": [
    ("Day one on a paver patio job: layout and excavation",
     "<p>We start by staking the patio's actual footprint against the house, string lines pulled tight so the excavation depth accounts for base, bedding sand and paver thickness all at once rather than guessing and correcting later. Excavation on a {city} lot usually goes a few inches past the planned finished grade to leave room for that full section, and we haul spoil off site the same day rather than stockpiling it on the lawn, since piled fill kills grass fast in Florida heat. By the end of day one the subgrade is cut, sloped and ready for whatever compaction the soil underneath needs.</p>"),
    ("Compacting the base before a single paver goes down",
     "<p>Aggregate base goes in 4- to 6-inch lifts, each one run over with a plate compactor heavy enough to reach 98 percent of standard Proctor density before the next lift is added, not after the whole pile is dumped and leveled at once. Skipping lift-by-lift compaction on a {city} patio is the single most common shortcut that shows up later as a low spot or a soft corner. We check compaction with a simple proof-roll, walking or rolling the compactor over the finished base and watching for any flex, before moving on to bedding sand the following day.</p>"),
    ("Screeding sand and setting pavers",
     "<p>Once the base passes inspection, we screed bedding sand to a uniform 1 inch using pipe rails as a guide, then set pavers straight off the base without compacting that sand layer first, since compacting it before the units are down would leave the sand too dense for the pavers to settle in evenly. Laying typically runs from a fixed edge, like the house wall, outward toward the restraint line, with border pieces cut and set last once the field pattern is established. On a {city} patio of average size this stage usually takes a full day or two depending on the pattern and how much cutting the layout requires.</p>"),
    ("Locking the joints and setting the edge restraint",
     "<p>With the field laid, we sweep dry joint sand across the surface and run a plate compactor fitted with a protective pad over the whole patio two or three passes, which both seats the pavers into the bedding sand and drives sand up into the joints. Edge restraint goes in along the perimeter before or during this step, spiked into the base rather than the sand. A final sweep clears excess sand off the paver faces so it doesn't haze in the Florida sun. On a {city} job, this compaction and joint-sand stage is usually the last full working day before cleanup.</p>"),
    ("Scheduling a patio job around the rainy season",
     "<p>Central and southwest Florida both see a rainy season running roughly from late May into October, often arriving as a sharp afternoon storm rather than steady rain, which changes how a crew sequences the work. We try to get base compaction and bedding sand finished before midday on a {city} job scheduled during those months, since a sudden downpour on exposed, uncompacted aggregate can wash fines out and force a section to be redone. Paver laying itself tolerates a light rain better than the base stage does, but joint-sand sweeping and compacting still wants a dry window to lock the sand in properly.</p>"),
    ("How a walkway job differs from a full patio build",
     "<p>A connecting walkway or a run of stepping pavers moves faster than a full patio because the footprint is smaller and the layout is usually a single straight or gently curved line rather than a shape with multiple cut angles. On a {city} property, a straightforward front-walk job can go from excavation to swept joints in a single day, where a comparable square footage of open patio with a border pattern takes closer to two. The base and compaction standards don't change for the smaller job, only the time it takes to move through each stage of it.</p>"),
    ("Tying new paver work into an existing slab mid-project",
     "<p>When a patio extends off an existing concrete pad, the saw-cutting and elevation matching happen early, usually on day one, so every later stage, base compaction, sand screeding, paver laying, is built around a confirmed, flush transition rather than guessed at and corrected at the end. We dry-lay a row of pavers against the cut edge before compacting the final base lift, checking the finished height against the old slab with a straightedge. On a {city} addition this extra verification step adds maybe an hour to the job and prevents the far more time-consuming fix of pulling up a finished section later.</p>"),
    ("The last day: inspection, cleanup and the final walk",
     "<p>If the jurisdiction reviewing the job requires a permit or inspection for the patio, that typically happens after base compaction and before pavers are set, so the base stays visible for the inspector. Once joints are swept and compacted, we do a final walk across the whole surface checking for any paver that rocks or sits proud, resetting it in its sand bed before calling the job finished rather than after. On a {city} patio, cleanup also means clearing excess joint sand and any base material off adjacent grass or planting beds, since leftover aggregate smothers turf within a couple of weeks.</p>"),
  ],
  "risks": [
    ("What happens when the base is skipped or under-compacted",
     "<p>The most common failure on a paver patio isn't a cracked unit, it's a low spot where the base underneath was never compacted to spec. On flatwoods soils with a seasonal high water table within 18 inches of the surface, an under-compacted base settles unevenly as the ground saturates and dries through the year, and the pavers simply follow it down. A {city} patio built on a thin or skipped base might look fine the day it's finished and show the first dish-shaped low spot after one full rainy season. There's no fix for this short of pulling the affected pavers, correcting the base and resetting them.</p>"),
    ("Ants and weeds moving into the joints",
     "<p>Dry joint sand is an easy place for ants to excavate a nest and for windblown weed seed to germinate, especially in joints that were never compacted tight or that have washed thin over time. On a {city} patio in a shaded or irrigated corner, both problems tend to show up together, since loose, damp sand is friendlier to ants and seedlings than sand that's been properly compacted and kept topped off. Re-sanding and compacting the joints removes the loose material both are living in, and it's a maintenance task, not a sign the patio was built wrong, though neglected joints make it worse.</p>"),
    ("That white haze on new pavers: efflorescence",
     "<p>New concrete pavers can develop a whitish, chalky film called efflorescence, a calcium carbonate deposit that can appear within 60 days of installation and sometimes doesn't show at all until the paver's first wet season if it was laid during a dry stretch. It does not affect the strength or durability of the paver itself. On a {city} patio, the bloom typically fades with normal weathering and rinsing over time, and sealing too early can sometimes trap it rather than help it wear off. It's worth knowing this before the first efflorescence shows up, because it looks alarming and almost never is.</p>"),
    ("Mold and algae on a shaded patio",
     "<p>A patio tucked under a tree canopy or on the north side of a house stays damp longer after each rain, and that moisture, combined with organic debris like leaf litter and pollen, feeds mold and algae growth on the paver surface. Beyond the look of it, a green or black film on a walking surface gets slick, and a shaded {city} patio near a pool or a lanai door is exactly where a slip matters most. Good drainage through the base and joints helps the surface dry faster between rains, but a shaded patio will still need more frequent cleaning than one in full sun.</p>"),
    ("Tree roots lifting a patio years after installation",
     "<p>A large tree planted or left standing near a patio will eventually send roots under the paver field looking for water, and a root thick enough to matter can lift an entire section a visible amount over a season or two. Some cities in this part of Florida protect legacy live oaks by ordinance, so cutting a structural root on a {city} property near a protected tree can require a permit or an arborist's sign-off before any repair starts. Pavers make this problem easier to live with than a poured slab would, since lifting the affected units, addressing the root and resetting them doesn't mean breaking anything.</p>"),
    ("Settling versus a sinkhole: telling the difference",
     "<p>A sunken corner of a patio unsettles people faster than it should, and in the vast majority of cases it's ordinary settling, not a sinkhole. True ground-cover collapse is a sudden, dramatic event tied to structural damage, while a paver patio dishing an inch over a year is almost always a base that lost compaction or washed out at an edge. Even counties on the state's higher sinkhole-claim list see settling far more often than an actual collapse, and counties like Sarasota and Manatee aren't on that list at all. A {city} homeowner with a low spot should expect a base repair, not a geological survey.</p>"),
    ("Downspouts and sprinkler overspray undermining the edge",
     "<p>A gutter downspout aimed at the patio edge, or an irrigation head throwing water against the same spot every cycle, concentrates far more water on one section of base than the rest of the patio ever sees. Over time that washes fines out from under the nearest pavers, and the edge restraint, which relies on solid compacted base to hold its spikes, loses its grip. On a {city} patio bordered by a sprinkler zone, redirecting the head or adding a splash block costs little compared with resetting a section of pavers that settled because the ground under it was quietly eroding for two seasons.</p>"),
    ("Treating landscape edging as a real edge restraint",
     "<p>Plastic or metal edging sold for flower beds is not rated to hold back a paver field, but it gets substituted in on budget jobs often enough that ICPI's own technical guidance specifically calls it out as unacceptable. Without a proper restraint spiked into the compacted base, the outer courses of a {city} patio creep outward a little at a time, usually from weight shifting as people walk the perimeter or furniture gets dragged across it, and the joints near the edge start opening within a year or two. Replacing bed edging with a real restraint after the fact means pulling and resetting the whole border course.</p>"),
  ],
  "care": [
    ("Re-sanding paver joints before they open up",
     "<p>Joint sand works its way out over time through rain, foot traffic and the odd ant colony excavating a channel, and once it drops much below the top of the paver, the units can rock and the joint edges start to chip. Topping off joint sand and compacting it back in is routine upkeep, not a repair, and catching it before the joints are visibly open is what keeps it that way. On a well-used {city} patio we'd expect this every couple of years, sooner in high-traffic zones near a door or a grill than in a quiet corner.</p>"),
    ("When to reseal a paver patio",
     "<p>ICPI's own technical guidance gives no fixed resealing interval for paver sealers, saying only that they need reapplication after a period of wear and weather and that acrylic sealers generally last a few years before a recoat is due. Rather than sealing on a calendar, the better signal on a {city} patio is watching the surface: water that used to bead now soaks in, or the color has gone flat and faded, both mean the old coat has worn through. A paver patio that's never been sealed can go years without a problem, since sealing is about appearance and stain resistance more than structural need.</p>"),
    ("Cleaning mold and algae off a shaded patio",
     "<p>A shaded or damp {city} patio needs more frequent cleaning than one in full sun, since mold and algae regrow fast once conditions favor them. A mild cleaner made for pavers, applied with a stiff brush and rinsed well, handles most of it without disturbing the joint sand the way a close-range pressure washer will. High-pressure washing blasted directly into the joints strips sand out faster than normal weathering does, which trades one maintenance problem for another. Trimming back overhanging branches to let more light and air reach the surface does more for long-term algae control than any cleaner applied after the fact.</p>"),
    ("Keeping ants and weeds out of the joints",
     "<p>Compacted, properly topped-off joint sand is the simplest defense against both ants nesting and weed seed germinating between pavers, since loose or thin sand gives both something to work with. A quick sweep to clear organic debris like leaf litter and grass clippings off a {city} patio before it decomposes into the joints also removes the organic matter weeds actually need to sprout. For an established ant problem in the joints, treating the colony directly works better than repeatedly re-sanding over it, since the ants will just re-excavate fresh sand the same way they did the old.</p>"),
    ("Living with efflorescence while it fades",
     "<p>The whitish bloom that shows up on new concrete pavers within the first couple of months is cosmetic, and the most effective response on most {city} patios is patience: normal rain and foot traffic wear it down over a season or two without any special treatment. A mild efflorescence cleaner can speed that up if the look bothers you sooner, but scrubbing too aggressively on a fresh install can do more harm than waiting it out. Sealing over an active efflorescence bloom is worth avoiding, since trapping the deposit under a sealer coat can make it harder to clear later rather than easier.</p>"),
    ("Caring for travertine differently than concrete pavers",
     "<p>Travertine's porous surface takes in water, oils and tannin stains from leaves and pine straw faster than a denser concrete paver does, so a {city} patio in travertine benefits from a sealer that's actually maintained on schedule rather than treated as optional. It also stays noticeably cooler underfoot in direct sun, part of why homeowners choose it around a pool, though we won't attach an unverified number to that difference. Day to day, the cleaning routine is the same as concrete, a mild cleaner and a regular rinse, just with less tolerance for letting stains sit before treating them.</p>"),
    ("Upkeep on a walkway versus a full patio",
     "<p>A narrow connecting walkway sees concentrated foot traffic along the same path every day, so its joints and bedding sand near the center wear faster than the edges, unlike a patio where wear spreads out more evenly across furniture zones and open space. Individual stepping pavers set in a lawn are even simpler to maintain, since a single unit that settles or tilts can be lifted and reset in its sand bed without touching anything around it. On a {city} property with both a walkway and a patio, expect the walkway's joints to need attention more often even though it's the smaller surface.</p>"),
    ("A seasonal rinse before the rainy season hits",
     "<p>A paver patio collects pollen, leaf litter and wind-blown dirt year-round, and all of it works into the joints and feeds algae if it's left to sit. A straightforward rinse and sweep a few times a year, timed before the rainy season ramps up from late May into October, keeps that buildup from compounding into a bigger cleaning job later. On a {city} patio under any tree cover, clearing debris off the surface regularly also protects the joint sand underneath it, since decomposing organic matter breaks sand down and gives weeds a foothold faster than clean sand ever would.</p>"),
  ],
}
